================================================================================
CS744 Lab 4 - Load Test 2: Pingpong Echo (I/O-bound)
================================================================================

1. AIM:
   How does throughput and response time scale for an I/O-bound lightweight request across server architectures under increasing concurrency?

2. SETUP & EXECUTION:
   Machine: Intel(R) Core(TM) i5-11400H @ 2.70GHz (6 physical cores, 12 hyperthreads)
   Server core assignment: taskset -c 0,1 (Physical Cores 0 and 1)
   Load generator (wrk) assignment: taskset -c 2,3,4,5 (Physical Cores 2, 3, 4, 5)
   Measurement duration: 10s per point
   Varying parameter: Concurrency -c over [1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128]
   Client threads: -t = min(c, 4)
   Metrics: Throughput (requests/sec) and Mean Response Time (ms)

3. HYPOTHESIS:
   With minimal CPU compute per request, throughput will reach high rates (60k-90k req/s). Thread pools of 2, 4, 8 will handle concurrent socket handoffs effectively. In thread-per-request mode, the kernel thread creation/destruction overhead per connection will become a dominant bottleneck compared to the trivial request execution time.

