6. OBSERVATIONS:
- Single-threaded (s): Scales rapidly from 19,595.16 req/s at c=1 to ~61,470.77 req/s at c=64.
- Thread-per-request (m): Reaches only ~30,877.67 req/s at c=4 and drops to ~23,658.91 req/s at c=64 and ~24,799.50 req/s at c=128. It is significantly outperformed by all other modes.
- Thread Pool 1 (t1): Scales from 19,601.61 req/s up to 76,200.34 req/s at c=16, then stabilizes around 60k req/s.
- Thread Pool 2 (t2): Scales steadily to 67,913.24 req/s at c=128 with low latency (1.260 ms).
- Thread Pool 4 (t4): Reaches 74,776.93 req/s at c=64 and 73,896.69 req/s at c=128.
- Thread Pool 8 (t8): Achieves the highest throughput, reaching 82,578.47 req/s at c=64 and maintaining 77,972.34 req/s at c=128 with only 0.890 ms mean latency.
- Response Time: Single-threaded latency increases to 2.600 ms at c=128, while t8 maintains sub-millisecond latency (0.890 ms) even under 128 concurrent connections.

7. EXPLANATION & INFERENCES:
- Lightweight Workload Behavior: Unlike the prime check, /pingpong requires almost zero CPU computation. The performance is dominated by network I/O, socket accept/recv/send system calls, and TCP connection teardown.
- Catastrophic Cost of Thread Creation: In thread-per-request mode, creating a new thread takes more CPU time than serving the actual pingpong response. Consequently, the CPU is overwhelmed with pthread_create and pthread_exit overhead, capping throughput at ~25k req/s.
- Thread Pool Advantage: Because request handling involves blocking I/O (epoll/recv/send handoffs), having multiple worker threads (4 and 8) allows overlapping network I/O and socket processing, achieving over 82.5k req/s.
